<td id="mwAnA"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./WI-38" title="WI-38" id="mwAnE">WI-38</a> human embryonic <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Cell_line" title="Cell line" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAnI">cell line</a></td>
<td id="mwAnM">MEM (solution containing buffered salts, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Fetal_bovine_serum" title="Fetal bovine serum" id="mwAnQ">fetal bovine serum</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Human_serum_albumin" title="Human serum albumin" id="mwAnU">human serum albumin</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Neomycin" title="Neomycin" id="mwAnY">neomycin</a>, etc.)</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p id="mwAnc">The term "MPR vaccine" is also used to refer to this vaccine, whereas "P" refers to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Parotitis" title="Parotitis" id="mwAng">parotitis</a> which is caused by mumps.<sup about="#mwt296" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-mpr_1-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"mpr"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-mpr-1" id="mwAnk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAno"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAns">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAn0">Merck MMR II is supplied freeze-dried (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Lyophilization" title="Lyophilization" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAn4">lyophilized</a>) and contains live viruses. Before injection, it is reconstituted with the solvent provided.<sup about="#mwt299" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-73" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-73"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-73" id="mwAn8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoE">[</span>73<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAoM">According to a review published in 2018, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./GlaxoSmithKline" title="GlaxoSmithKline" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAoQ">GlaxoSmithKline</a> (GSK) MMR vaccine known as Pluserix "contains the Schwarz measles virus, the Jeryl Lynn–like mumps strain, and RA27/3 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Rubella_virus" title="Rubella virus" id="mwAoU">rubella virus</a>".<sup about="#mwt302" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-plotkin18_74-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"plotkin18"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-plotkin18-74"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-plotkin18-74" id="mwAoY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAog">[</span>74<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAok">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAoo">Pluserix was introduced in Hungary in 1999.<sup about="#mwt303" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-borocz20_75-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"borocz20"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-borocz20-75" id="mwAos"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAow"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAo0">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAo4">]</span></span></a></sup> Enders' Edmonston strain has been used since 1999 in Hungary in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Merck_&amp;_Co." title="Merck &amp; Co." id="mwAo8">Merck</a> MMR II product.<sup about="#mwt306" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-borocz20_75-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"borocz20"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-borocz20-75"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-borocz20-75" id="mwApA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApI">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApM">]</span></span></a></sup> GSK Priorix vaccine, which uses attenuated Schwarz Measles, was introduced in Hungary in 2003.<sup about="#mwt307" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-borocz20_75-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"borocz20"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-borocz20-75" id="mwApQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApY">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="9" id="mwApg"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="MMRV_vaccine">MMRV vaccine</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt308" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"MMRV vaccine"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwApk"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt309" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt308" id="mwApo">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./MMRV_vaccine" title="MMRV vaccine">MMRV vaccine</a></div>

<p id="mwAps">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./MMRV_vaccine" title="MMRV vaccine" id="mwApw">MMRV vaccine</a>, a combined measles, mumps, rubella, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Varicella" title="Varicella" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAp0">varicella</a> (chickenpox) vaccine is used as a replacement for the MMR vaccine to simplify the administration of the vaccines.<sup about="#mwt312" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Vesikari_35-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Vesikari"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Vesikari-35"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-Vesikari-35" id="mwAp4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAp8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqA">[</span>35<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="10" id="mwAqI"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="MR_vaccine">MR vaccine</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt313" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Update section","href":"./Template:Update_section"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"November 2022"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAqM"><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1364180890" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt314" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Message box/ambox.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'>.mw-parser-output .ambox{border:1px solid #a2a9b1;border-left:10px solid #36c;background-color:#fbfbfb;box-sizing:border-box}.mw-parser-output .ambox+.mw-empty-elt+.ambox,.mw-parser-output .ambox+.mw-empty-elt+.mw-empty-elt+.ambox,.mw-parser-output .ambox+.mw-empty-elt+.mw-empty-elt+.mw-empty-elt+.ambox{margin-top:-1px}html body.mediawiki .mw-parser-output .ambox.mbox-small-left{margin:4px 1em 4px 0;overflow:hidden;width:238px;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:88%;line-height:1.25em}.mw-parser-output .ambox-speedy{border-left:10px solid #b32424;background-color:#fee7e6}.mw-parser-output .ambox-delete{border-left:10px solid #b32424}.mw-parser-output .ambox-content{border-left:10px solid #f28500}.mw-parser-output .ambox-style{border-left:10px solid #fc3}.mw-parser-output .ambox-move{border-left:10px solid #9932cc}.mw-parser-output .ambox-protection{border-left:10px solid #a2a9b1}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-text{border:none;padding:0.25em 0.5em;width:100%}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-image{border:none;padding:2px 0 2px 0.5em;text-align:center}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-imageright{border:none;padding:2px 0.5em 2px 0;text-align:center}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-empty-cell{border:none;padding:0;width:1px}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-image-div{width:52px}@media(min-width:720px){.mw-parser-output .ambox{margin:0 10%}}@media print{body.ns-0 .mw-parser-output .ambox{display:none!important}}</style></span><table class="box-Update plainlinks metadata ambox ambox-content ambox-Update" role="presentation" about="#mwt313"><tbody><tr><td class="mbox-image"><div class="mbox-image-div"><span typeof="mw:File"><span><img resource="./File:Ambox_current_red_Asia_Australia.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Ambox_current_red_Asia_Australia.svg/60px-Ambox_current_red_Asia_Australia.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Ambox_current_red_Asia_Australia.svg/120px-Ambox_current_red_Asia_Australia.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="" data-file-width="360" data-file-height="290" data-file-type="drawing" height="34" width="42" class="mw-file-element"/></span></span></div></td><td class="mbox-text"><div class="mbox-text-span">This section needs to be <b>updated</b>.<span class="hide-when-compact"> Please help update this article to reflect recent events or newly available information.</span>  <span class="date-container"><i>(<span class="date">November 2022</span>)</i></span></div></td></tr></tbody></table><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt313"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Wikipedia_articles_in_need_of_updating_from_November_2022"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_Wikipedia_articles_in_need_of_updating"/></span>

<p id="mwAqQ">The MR vaccine (abbreviated "MRV") is a vaccine for measles and rubella, not mumps.<sup about="#mwt315" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WHO2014Sheet_22-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WHO2014Sheet"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-WHO2014Sheet-22" id="mwAqU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqc">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqg">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2014, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./World_Health_Organization" title="World Health Organization" id="mwAqk">World Health Organization</a> reported it as used in a "few (unidentified) countries".<sup about="#mwt316" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WHO2014Sheet_22-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WHO2014Sheet"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-WHO2014Sheet-22" id="mwAqo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqw">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAq0">]</span></span></a></sup> A MRV delivered by a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Microneedle_patch" title="Microneedle patch" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAq4">microneedle patch</a> was the subject of a study reported in 2024.<sup about="#mwt319" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-76" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-76"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-76" id="mwAq8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArE">[</span>76<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="11" id="mwArM"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Society_and_culture">Society and culture</h2>
<section data-mw-section-id="12" id="mwArQ"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Religious_concerns">Religious concerns</h3>
<p id="mwArU">Some brands of the vaccine use <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Gelatin" title="Gelatin" id="mwArY">gelatin</a>, derived from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Pig" title="Pig" id="mwArc">pigs</a>, as a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Stabilizer_(chemistry)" title="Stabilizer (chemistry)" id="mwArg">stabilizer</a>.<sup about="#mwt320" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PHE_77-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PHE"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-PHE-77" id="mwArk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAro"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArs">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArw">]</span></span></a></sup> This has caused reduced take-up among some communities,<sup about="#mwt321" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PHE_77-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PHE"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-PHE-77" id="mwAr0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAr4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAr8">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt324" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-78" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-78"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-78" id="mwAsE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsM">[</span>78<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsQ">]</span></span></a></sup> despite the fact that alternative vaccines without pig derivatives are approved and available.<sup about="#mwt327" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PHE_77-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PHE"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-PHE-77"}}'><a href="./MMR_vaccine#cite_note-PHE-77" id="mwAsU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsc">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

